part of 'course_content.dart';
const extendedTopics = <Topic>[
Topic("python-c1-t0",1,"Comparisons and Boolean values",goal:"Understand comparisons and boolean values, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Comparisons ask a yes/no question. == compares values; = assigns a name. A Boolean can be stored and reused. Check equal values and values on both sides of a boundary. Comparing text follows text rules, so convert numeric text before numeric comparisons.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"age = 17\nprint(age >= 18)\nprint(age == 17)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c1-t1",1,"if, elif, and else",goal:"Understand if, elif, and else, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"An if statement chooses a branch using a condition. elif tries another condition only when earlier conditions were false; else covers the remaining cases. Exactly one branch in an if/elif/else chain runs. The order matters when conditions overlap.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"score = 65\nif score >= 80:\n    print(\"High\")\nelif score >= 50:\n    print(\"Pass\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Review\")",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c1-t2",1,"and, or, and not",goal:"Understand and, or, and not, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"and requires both Boolean conditions to hold; or needs at least one; not reverses a Boolean. Parentheses can make a combined rule easier to read. Python short-circuits these operations, so the second expression may not be evaluated. Start by writing a truth table for a small rule.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"has_ticket = True\nis_open = False\nprint(has_ticket and is_open)\nprint(has_ticket or is_open)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c1-t3",1,"Boundary values and decision tables",goal:"Understand boundary values and decision tables, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A boundary is a value where a decision changes. Testing a typical value alone can miss a wrong comparison operator. Test just below, exactly at, and just above each boundary. A decision table lists inputs and their expected outcomes before code is written.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"for value in [9, 10, 11]:\n    print(value, value >= 10)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c1-t4",1,"Nested conditions",goal:"Understand nested conditions, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A nested condition makes a second decision inside a branch of the first. Indentation tells Python which branch owns each statement. Deep nesting is harder to follow, so consider early returns or combined conditions when they express the same rule clearly. Trace one complete route at a time.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"member = True\ncredit = 4\nif member:\n    if credit > 0:\n        print(\"Ready\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Add credit\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Join first\")",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c1-t5",1,"Truthiness and match statements",goal:"Understand truthiness and match statements, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Empty collections and strings are false in a Boolean context; non-empty ones are true. Numeric zero and None are also false. Truthiness is different from checking a specific value. match selects a pattern; a final wildcard case can provide a default. Use these tools when their meaning stays clear.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"items = []\nprint(bool(items))\nitems.append(\"book\")\nprint(bool(items))\nmatch \"help\":\n    case \"help\":\n        print(\"Show guidance\")\n    case _:\n        print(\"Unknown\")",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c2-t0",2,"while loops and termination",goal:"Understand while loops and termination, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A while loop repeats while its condition is true. Its state must move towards termination unless the loop is intentionally controlled in another way. Record the condition, state before the body, and state after it. Stop is useful while experimenting with a loop that does not terminate.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"remaining = 3\nwhile remaining > 0:\n    print(remaining)\n    remaining -= 1\nprint(\"Done\")",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c2-t1",2,"for loops and range",goal:"Understand for loops and range, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A for loop requests each item from an iterable. range(start, stop, step) generates integers and excludes stop. A range can be empty. Count expected iterations before running, especially when using a negative step or a starting value other than zero.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"for number in range(2, 7, 2):\n    print(number)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c2-t2",2,"Lists and iteration",goal:"Understand lists and iteration, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A list keeps items in order and allows duplicates. Indexing selects one item; iteration visits items in order. Appending changes the same list. Avoid adding or removing items from the list you are currently traversing unless you understand the changing positions. A separate result list is often clearer.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"names = [\"Amina\", \"Musa\", \"Amina\"]\nfor name in names:\n    print(name)\nprint(names[1])",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c2-t3",2,"Dictionaries and iteration",goal:"Understand dictionaries and iteration, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A dictionary associates a unique key with a value. Iterating the dictionary visits keys; items() supplies key/value pairs. A missing direct lookup raises KeyError, while get can provide a default. Choose a dictionary when lookup by a meaningful key matters more than position.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"scores = {\"Amina\": 8, \"Musa\": 6}\nfor name, score in scores.items():\n    print(name, score)\nprint(scores.get(\"Sara\", 0))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c2-t4",2,"Counters and accumulators",goal:"Understand counters and accumulators, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A counter counts qualifying events; an accumulator combines values into a running result. Choose a suitable initial value: zero for addition, one for multiplication, and an empty collection for collected items. Trace the value after each iteration and consider an empty input.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"values = [4, -1, 7]\ncount = 0\ntotal = 0\nfor value in values:\n    if value > 0:\n        count += 1\n        total += value\nprint(count, total)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c3-t0",3,"Understanding an exception traceback",goal:"Understand understanding an exception traceback, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A traceback describes the chain of calls that led to an exception. Start with the final exception name and message, then find the relevant learner.py line. A syntax error prevents the program from being parsed; a runtime error occurs while valid syntax executes. Repair the cause rather than only hiding the message.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"try:\n    print(int(\"hello\"))\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(type(error).__name__)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c3-t1",3,"Recovering from invalid numeric input",goal:"Understand recovering from invalid numeric input, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Put only the operation that may fail inside a try block where possible. Catch the specific exception you expect. A failed conversion does not produce a usable number. Decide what the caller should receive on failure; retrying input is a separate interface decision.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"for text in [\"12\", \"bad\"]:\n    try:\n        value = int(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        print(\"Invalid number\")\n    else:\n        print(value * 2)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c3-t2",3,"Raising and propagating errors",goal:"Understand raising and propagating errors, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A function can raise an exception when an input violates its contract. If it does not catch that exception, the exception propagates to its caller. Catching at the interface can give a useful message while calculation functions remain clear. Do not catch every exception merely to label every error as invalid input.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"def positive(value):\n    if value <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a positive value\")\n    return value\n\ntry:\n    positive(0)\nexcept ValueError as error:\n    print(error)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c4-t0",4,"Importing a standard library module",goal:"Understand importing a standard library module, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Importing a module binds a name through which its public functions and values can be used. Standard-library modules come with Python, but you still import them explicitly. Use qualified names when they make the origin of a function clear. Avoid naming your own file after the module you intend to import.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"import math\nprint(math.isqrt(20))\nprint(math.ceil(2.3))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c4-t1",4,"Choosing an API and reading its documentation",goal:"Understand choosing an api and reading its documentation, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"An API describes how to use an operation: its inputs, result, exceptions and side effects. Check the documentation and a small example before building a larger feature. A function name alone does not tell you every rule. Test a normal case and an edge case, and distinguish deterministic results from random ones.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"import statistics\nvalues = [2, 8, 5]\nprint(statistics.median(values))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c4-t2",4,"Isolating project dependencies",goal:"Understand isolating project dependencies, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A dependency is a package your program relies on. A virtual environment isolates installed packages for a project on a normal Python installation. Keep package names and versions documented. This browser terminal has its own runtime: installing packages on your laptop does not install them here. Standard-library examples can run without a separate package installation.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"import importlib.util\nprint(importlib.util.find_spec(\"json\") is not None)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c5-t0",5,"Checking a function with assert",goal:"Understand checking a function with assert, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"An assertion checks a condition and raises AssertionError when it is false. Test a function using independent expectations, rather than repeating its implementation. Keep calculation functions separate from printing so their return values can be compared directly. Assertions are useful for tests, not for validating untrusted input in production.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"def triple(value):\n    return value * 3\n\nassert triple(4) == 12\nassert triple(0) == 0\nprint(\"Checks passed\")",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c5-t1",5,"Testing boundaries and failure cases",goal:"Understand testing boundaries and failure cases, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A useful test suite includes boundaries, empty cases, negative values when allowed, and failures that should raise a specific exception. A passing typical example does not prove every input works. State the expected result before running the test. For decimal calculations, a stated numeric tolerance can be more meaningful than identical formatting.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"def allowed(age):\n    return age >= 18\n\nfor age, expected in [(17, False), (18, True), (19, True)]:\n    assert allowed(age) == expected\nprint(\"Boundary checks passed\")",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c5-t2",5,"Writing an independent test suite",goal:"Understand writing an independent test suite, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Tests should be readable evidence of a contract. Give each test a clear purpose and keep unrelated cases separate. A reference implementation and a test serve different roles: the reference shows one solution, while the test checks externally observable behaviour. The terminal can run standard unittest tests; third-party test tools may need an additional package.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"import unittest\n\nclass ArithmeticTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_total(self):\n        self.assertEqual(sum([2, 3]), 5)\n\nsuite = unittest.defaultTestLoader.loadTestsFromTestCase(ArithmeticTests)\nresult = unittest.TestResult()\nsuite.run(result)\nprint(result.wasSuccessful())",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c6-t0",6,"Opening, reading and closing a text file",goal:"Understand opening, reading and closing a text file, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A file holds data beyond one calculation. Use a with block so the file closes even if an error occurs. Choose the correct mode: reading does not create a missing file; writing replaces existing contents; appending adds data. In this learning terminal each run has a fresh temporary filesystem. Download important files or code before ending a session.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"with open(\"message.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    file.write(\"Hello\\nPython\\n\")\n\nwith open(\"message.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    print(file.read(), end=\"\")",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c6-t1",6,"Writing structured records",goal:"Understand writing structured records, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Structured formats give fields and records a defined representation. CSV is useful for rows; JSON represents values such as lists and dictionaries. Use the standard parser rather than splitting complex CSV text manually. Decide how numbers will be represented, because a CSV reader normally returns text fields.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"import json\nrecord = {\"name\": \"Amina\", \"minutes\": 30}\ntext = json.dumps(record)\nrestored = json.loads(text)\nprint(restored[\"minutes\"])",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c6-t2",6,"Validating loaded data",goal:"Understand validating loaded data, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Parsing is only the first step: valid JSON can still contain the wrong type or missing fields. Validate the shape before using the data. State which values are allowed and what should happen on failure. Keep the original data intact until a complete validation succeeds.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"import json\nrecord = json.loads(\"{\\\"minutes\\\": 30}\")\nvalid = isinstance(record, dict) and type(record.get(\"minutes\")) is int\nprint(valid)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c7-t0",7,"Matching a simple text pattern",goal:"Understand matching a simple text pattern, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A regular expression describes a text pattern. Literal characters match themselves; character classes describe permitted characters. Some symbols have special meaning and must be escaped when you mean the symbol itself. Use raw strings for many Python patterns so backslashes are easier to read. Start with a small pattern and test what it accepts and rejects.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"import re\nfor text in [\"123\", \"12x\", \"\"]:\n    print(bool(re.fullmatch(r\"[0-9]+\", text)))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c7-t1",7,"Searching and extracting a group",goal:"Understand searching and extracting a group, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"search looks for a match anywhere in the text; fullmatch requires the whole text to match. A capturing group lets you extract part of a match. A search can return None, so check before accessing a group. Decide whether you need the first match or every match.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"import re\nmatch = re.search(r\"minutes=([0-9]+)\", \"name=Amina minutes=30\")\nif match:\n    print(match.group(1))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c7-t2",7,"Testing a pattern against edge cases",goal:"Understand testing a pattern against edge cases, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A pattern can accept more than you intended. Test empty text, unwanted extra characters, and boundary lengths. Anchor the match to the intended scope. A regex can check a format, but checking meaning may need ordinary Python rules too. Keep complicated validation readable rather than forcing every rule into one pattern.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"import re\npattern = r\"[A-Z]{2}[0-9]{3}\"\nfor text in [\"AB123\", \"A123\", \"AB1234\"]:\n    print(bool(re.fullmatch(pattern, text)))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c8-t0",8,"Defining a class and creating objects",goal:"Understand defining a class and creating objects, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A class defines behaviour and data for objects. Calling the class creates an instance. __init__ initialises that instance; self refers to the particular object receiving a method call. Different instances can hold different state while sharing the same method definitions.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"class Learner:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n    def greet(self):\n        return f\"Hello, {self.name}\"\n\nprint(Learner(\"Amina\").greet())",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c8-t1",8,"Separating instance data from class data",goal:"Understand separating instance data from class data, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"An instance attribute belongs to one object; a class attribute is shared through the class. A mutable list placed on the class can accidentally become shared state. Put per-object collections in __init__. Test two instances so that changing one cannot silently change the other.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"class Notebook:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.notes = []\n\na = Notebook()\nb = Notebook()\na.notes.append(\"Practice\")\nprint(a.notes)\nprint(b.notes)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c8-t2",8,"Designing a small object interface",goal:"Understand designing a small object interface, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"An interface is the set of operations callers can rely on. Keep those operations clear and maintain rules about valid state. A method can validate a request before changing the object. Prefer a small useful interface to many methods that expose every internal detail. Describe side effects and return values separately.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 0\n    def add(self, amount):\n        if amount < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Negative amount\")\n        self.value += amount\n\ncounter = Counter()\ncounter.add(3)\nprint(counter.value)",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c9-t0",9,"Comprehensions and generators",goal:"Understand comprehensions and generators, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A comprehension builds a collection from an iterable, with an optional filter. A generator produces values lazily instead of building the whole list first. Use these forms when they stay readable; a normal loop is often clearer for several steps or side effects. Compare a generator with a list and notice that consuming it advances its state.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"squares = [n * n for n in range(4)]\nprint(squares)\nvalues = (n * n for n in range(4))\nprint(next(values))\nprint(list(values))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c9-t1",9,"Describing interfaces with type hints",goal:"Understand describing interfaces with type hints, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Type hints describe an expected interface for readers and analysis tools. Python normally does not enforce them automatically during execution. A hint is not a replacement for input validation. State the contract in plain language as well: allowed values, return value and possible failures.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"def total(values: list[int]) -> int:\n    return sum(values)\n\nprint(total([2, 3, 4]))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c9-t2",9,"Refactoring a program without changing its behaviour",goal:"Understand refactoring a program without changing its behaviour, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Refactoring changes the internal organisation while keeping the public behaviour the same. Record existing behaviour and tests before restructuring. Extract repeated calculations, improve names, and rerun the tests after each small change. A change that alters results is a behaviour change and needs its own decision.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"def price(quantity, unit_cost):\n    return quantity * unit_cost\n\nprint(f\"Total: {price(3, 2.5):.2f}\")",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c10-t0",10,"Planning a final project",goal:"Understand planning a final project, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A project begins with a bounded problem and a user who needs a result. Write inputs, outputs, rules, and examples before choosing functions. Separate the calculation from input/output. Make a small working version first, then add features in steps. A plan should include how you will know that each feature works.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"def total_sessions(minutes):\n    return sum(minutes)\n\nexample_sessions = [20, 30, 10]\nprint(total_sessions(example_sessions))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c10-t1",10,"Collecting evidence and tests",goal:"Understand collecting evidence and tests, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"Evidence makes a project understandable to someone else. Keep a concise requirement list, representative tests, failure cases, and an explanation of important choices. Record assistance honestly. A screenshot shows one run; a reproducible test shows how to check the behaviour again. Collect both automatic results and reviewed explanations.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"def total(values):\n    return sum(values)\n\nchecks = [([], 0), ([2, 3], 5), ([-2, 2], 0)]\nfor values, expected in checks:\n    assert total(values) == expected\nprint(\"Three evidence checks passed\")",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
Topic("python-c10-t2",10,"Final assessment and reflection",goal:"Understand final assessment and reflection, test an example, and apply it independently.",lesson:"A final reflection should identify what you can do independently, where evidence is weak, and the next useful practice task. Revisit unfamiliar inputs instead of only repeating memorised examples. Full-course certification requires published requirements and assessment review; completing practice terminals alone cannot issue it. This lesson is preparation, not the final examination.\n\nRead the example before running it. Predict the result and record why. Run it in the terminal below, compare your prediction with the result, then change one value. Identify which behaviour stays the same and which changes.\n\nCheck your understanding: explain the rule in your own words, use a different input, and describe one situation where the rule matters. Save your explanation in My Notes.",example:"def average(values):\n    return sum(values) / len(values) if values else None\n\nprint(average([10, 20, 30]))\nprint(average([]))",output:"Run the example and compare its output with your prediction."),
];
